{
 "id": "elbow-link-fatigue-fdm",
 "brief": "Forearm link for my desktop robot arm, PA-CF (carbon-fibre nylon) on an FDM printer, printed flat\nwith layers along +Z. The elbow servo's 25 mm aluminium horn has its face at z = 0 and its axis on Z\nthrough the origin; the link sits on that face and is held by four M3 socket screws on a 16 mm\ncircle (at (8,0), (-8,0), (0,8), (0,-8)) through 3.4 mm holes, heads on top of a 5 mm thick hub (z =\n0 to 5), each with a 7.5 mm diameter column kept clear above it for the head and the hex key. Keep a\n6 mm hole clear on the axis (z = 0 up) for the horn's centre screw. The wrist end, 100 mm out along\n+X, carries two 10 mm OD bearings on a vertical axis at (100, 0): a 10 mm bore through z = 0 to 12\nwith at least 3 mm of wall round it. The wrist cable goes through an 8 mm vertical hole at (50, 0).\nThe link fits in x = -16 to 116, y = -16 to 16, z = 0 to 12. The elbow axis is horizontal, so the\nwrist's 0.8 kg swings in the link's plane (along -Y) on every move, at 2 g: that is 2 million cycles\nover its life, and I want a fatigue safety factor of 1.5 on it. It must also survive the elbow servo\nstalling at 2.5 N·m with the wrist jammed (once, not cycled) and a 10 N push on the wrist bearings\nalong the axis. Static safety factor 2, as light as you can, under 16 g.",
